# version, date-released and doi are left out on purpose. No DOI has been
# minted, and a version here would go stale with every release candidate;
# cite the version or commit you used, as the message says. Add them when a
# release is archived with a DOI.
#
# license lists the two licences of the package as distributed, matching
# package.json's SPDX expression "MIT AND CC-BY-4.0": the code is MIT, and
# the ΔT table values it ships are CC BY 4.0. Both apply together. The
# Citation File Format reads a list of licences as a choice between them
# (OR); that is not meant here. LICENSING.md gives the details. atlas/ and
# conformance/ carry their own licences: atlas/LICENSING.md and
# conformance/LICENSE.
cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below, with the version or commit you used. The time atlas in atlas/ has its own citation in atlas/README.md."
type: software
title: "Zodiacs Engine (@zodiacs/engine)"
abstract: >-
  TypeScript astrology calculations for browsers and Node.js: tropical
  planetary positions, natal charts with houses and aspects, transits,
  synastry, secondary progressions, Moon phase and Saturn returns; local
  birth times resolved on the tz database's history, and instants read as
  UTC, UT1 or TT; Hellenistic timing techniques; and the sidereal zodiac
  with Jyotish techniques. The repository also holds a conformance suite for
  astrology computation (test vectors, each decided by an independent
  arbiter with a stated tolerance, and a harness that runs any engine
  against them) and a historical time atlas of what civil clocks showed,
  where and when, with a primary source for every rule.
authors:
  - name: "Zodiacs.org"
    website: "https://zodiacs.org"
repository-code: "https://github.com/zodiacs-org/engine"
url: "https://zodiacs.org/developers/engine/"
license:
  - MIT
  - CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - astrology
  - ephemeris
  - natal chart
  - house systems
  - synastry
  - transits
  - TypeScript
  - conformance testing
